#!/usr/bin/env bash
# skill-drift - does a skill still describe a system that exists?
#
# A SKILL.md is prose an agent OBEYS. When a path in it moves, nothing fails
# loudly: the agent opens a file that is not there, improvises, and the session
# degrades silently. That is the same class as the LiteLLM/Ollama stale-SETUP
# incident (an email-triage skill classified against a host decommissioned
# months earlier, failing closed to "everything FYI" with no error) and as
# dotfiles-drift's merged-is-not-deployed gap.
#
# It was written after `update-rules` was found pointing EVERY path it named at
# ~/.dotfiles/, months after the rule layer moved to the ~/.dotfiles-private
# overlay. Six workflows, all opening files that did not exist. Its own built-in
# self-check had the same bug, so it could not report itself.
#
# The checks split into two families, and the split is what lets CI run this.
#
# STATIC - decidable from a repo checkout alone, so they gate every PR (--lint). They
# enforce the standard in ~/.local/src/docs/skills.md, which is the one home for the
# rules; this file holds only the mechanism.
#
#   NOFRONTMATTER  no YAML frontmatter, or no name:/description:.
#   BADYAML    the frontmatter does not parse, so Claude Code drops every field.
#   BADNAME    name is not kebab-case, or does not match its directory. A
#              mismatch is invisible until the wrong thing is invoked.
#   DUPNAME    the same skill name defined twice. ~/.claude/skills is ONE flat
#              namespace across both repos, so a duplicate is ambiguous.
#   BADMETA    metadata.category / tags / reviewed missing or malformed, a
#              category outside the known set, or a category that disagrees with
#              the parent directory.
#   CATCOLLIDE a category sharing a name with a skill. `comms/comms/` makes
#              `git mv` refuse, and the first attempt at the category migration
#              silently moved two sibling skills INSIDE the comms skill.
#   LONGDESC   description + when_to_use over 300 bytes, measured on the PARSED
#              value (a `>-` or `|` block counts its folded text, not its raw
#              lines). The session's skill listing has one shared budget of
#              about 30 KB for every skill and command; past it the rest arrive
#              as a bare name, so a long description costs other skills theirs.
#   NONASCII   the house plain-ASCII rule (em/en dash, arrows, >=, ellipsis...).
#   NOHEADING  no `## Scope and triggers` heading, so a reader cannot find what the
#              skill covers in the same place in every skill.
#   ARGSUB     a `$N` in the body. Claude Code replaces it with argument N when the
#              skill is called with that many arguments, so awk '{print $1}' or
#              "costs $0" turn into argument text. It has no escape for `$N`.
#
# .claude/commands/**/*.md and .claude/agents/*.md are linted too, as command:<path>
# and agent:<name>: NOFRONTMATTER, BADYAML, LONGDESC and NONASCII.
#
# NONASCII, NOHEADING and every command or agent finding can be frozen in
# tests/skill-lint.baseline; all other static findings are hard errors. --ratchet is
# what makes that file shrink-only, and --lint names any line whose debt is gone.
#
# ENVIRONMENTAL - need a real machine, so they stay local (report/--quiet/--json):
#
#   DEADPATH   a ~/ or $HOME/ path named in a SKILL.md does not exist. This is
#              the update-rules class and the reason this script exists.
#   NOCMD      a command the skill tells you to RUN does not resolve on PATH.
#              (`rulesync` lives at ~/.rulesync/bin/rulesync, never on PATH.)
#              A name the same doc defines as a shell function is not a command.
#   UNDEPLOYED a skill dir exists in a repo but has no link in ~/.claude/skills,
#              so it is documented and unreachable.
#   GHOST      CLAUDE.md bullets a skill that is not on disk (retired but still
#              advertised, so the agent tries to invoke it).
#   STALE      metadata.reviewed is older than STALE_DAYS. Not "last edited",
#              which git already answers - last VERIFIED against reality.
#   UNUSED     no use in USAGE_DAYS of transcripts, by any of the six signals
#              `claude-recall stats` counts (USE_KINDS below). A skill nothing calls
#              still spends its description tokens in every session. This is a
#              SIGNAL, not a verdict: a release runbook fires per release and is
#              supposed to look idle.
#   RETIRE     UNUSED, and the skill declares metadata.replaces and nobody typed any of
#              those forms either: nobody does the work at all. Reported instead of UNUSED.
#   BYPASSED   sessions typed a form the skill's metadata.replaces names without using the
#              skill (claude-recall's bypass kind; the rows are hand_methods.py's).
#   LISTBUDGET every listed skill and command together are over the shared listing
#              budget, so the last ones arrive as a bare name. A skill with `paths:`
#              or `disable-model-invocation: true` is not listed. Needs both repos
#              deployed, so it lives here and not in --lint.
#
# Deliberately trivial and dependency-free: a checker that needs a working
# environment cannot report a broken one. CI runs --lint and the nightly docs-cleanup
# runner reads --json and --docs --json. The 12h sentinel watch on --quiet was deleted 2026-11-02: it was red
# for 84 days, 33 of its 53 NEW findings UNUSED (a signal, not drift) and the rest
# false positives, so nobody read it.
#
# Escape hatch: a line containing `skill-drift: ignore` is skipped entirely.
# Hostnames of the form *.example.internal are SANITIZATION for the public repo
# (see dotfiles-land), never drift - paths containing them are skipped.
#
# A BASELINE is what makes this adoptable. The first run found 60+ real findings
# across skills written over months; a watch that is red from birth is a watch
# nobody reads. `--write-baseline` freezes today's known debt, after which
# --quiet trips only on drift that is NEW. The full report always shows
# everything, baselined or not, so the debt stays visible instead of forgotten.
#
# Usage:
#   skill-drift                  human-readable report (everything)
#   skill-drift --quiet          print nothing; exit 1 on NEW drift only
#   skill-drift --json           machine-readable (everything, with "new": true|false)
#   skill-drift --new            human-readable, NEW findings only
#   skill-drift --write-baseline freeze current findings as accepted
#   skill-drift --lint [DIR] [--profile full|project] [--allow-empty]
#                                STATIC checks only, over DIR/.claude. What CI runs:
#                                no deployed tree, no private repo and no CLAUDE.md
#                                exist on a runner, so the environmental checks
#                                cannot even be attempted. `full` is the dotfiles
#                                corpus. `project` is a game repo: no metadata.* or
#                                category rules, and flat .claude/skills/<name>/.
#                                Finding nothing fails, unless --allow-empty says
#                                this repo tracks no skills on purpose.
#   skill-drift --write-lint-baseline [DIR] [--profile full|project]
#                                freeze the current frozen-eligible debt
#   skill-drift --ratchet [DIR] [BASE]
#                                exit 1 if tests/skill-lint.baseline gained a line
#                                since the merge base with BASE (default origin/main).
#                                A commit trailer `Skill-Baseline-Raise: <why>` in
#                                that range allows it: a new check has debt to freeze.
#   skill-drift --overrides [--json]
#                                the skill-usage toggle: the skillOverrides object that keeps
#                                "name-only" on the skills used in no more than
#                                SKILL_TOGGLE_MAX_SESSIONS (5) sessions in SKILL_TOGGLE_DAYS (30),
#                                by any of the six signals, and lists one again past twice that
#                                (the band stops a skill at the bar flipping nightly), from the settings
#                                file SKILL_SETTINGS (~/.claude/settings.json). A skill another
#                                SKILL.md or a CLAUDE.md layer names (routes to) is never hidden
#                                and never restored for that alone; one used past the bar is restored. A
#                                `paths:` or disable-model-invocation skill is never toggled,
#                                nor is an override value other than "name-only".
#                                Prints the hide and restore lists; --json adds the object. The
#                                nightly skill-toggle runner opens the PR (agentctl-skill-toggle).
#                                Exit 2 when no use is counted at all: no transcripts is not
#                                "nothing used". Exit 3 when a transcript root does not reach
#                                back the whole window (claude-recall stats "covered_from" after
#                                "since"): a skill used only before then would read as unused.
#   skill-drift --docs [--json]  DEADPATH and NOCMD over the GLOBAL docs instead of
#                                the skills: both CLAUDE.md layers, AGENTS.md,
#                                ~/.local/src/docs, the agentctl runbooks, every
#                                auto-memory file, anchor and lessons file. Each
#                                finding names the doc and its first line. Override
#                                the set with DRIFT_DOCS (newline-separated paths).
#                                The nightly docs-cleanup runner reads --docs --json.
#   skill-drift --dups [--json] [--check | --file PATH]
#                                RESTATED instructions: a line of 90+ chars in one home
#                                that shares 4-word shingles (Jaccard >= 0.3) with a line
#                                in another. Corpus: the --docs set plus every SKILL.md and
#                                the agentctl roles and prompts. Each pair prints both homes
#                                and the one that wins (higher rank in the agentctl conf's
#                                instruction-homes.json; equal rank: a session picks).
#                                Exit 0 with pairs, 2 on an empty corpus or no homes file.
#                                --check holds the pair count to dup_pairs_max in kpi-thresholds
#                                (the stored baseline; lower it as pairs are removed): exit 1
#                                with the pairs listed while the count is over it, 2 when the
#                                row is missing. --json adds max and over. --file PATH keeps the
#                                pairs that have a line in that file, exit 1 when there are any
#                                (the edit-time guard's question). Anchor/anchor pairs are not
#                                counted: every anchor is one generated template.
#                                AUTO:START/END blocks are skipped (a file with `<!-- shared: -->`
#                                markers holds none of the spliced text). A lexical floor: only
#                                near-verbatim lines are found, paraphrase is not.
set -uo pipefail

SKILLS="${CLAUDE_SKILLS:-$HOME/.claude/skills}"
PUB="${DOTFILES:-$HOME/.dotfiles}"
PRIV="${DOTFILES_PRIVATE:-$HOME/.dotfiles-private}"
COMMANDS="${CLAUDE_COMMANDS:-$HOME/.claude/commands}"
CLAUDE_MD="${CLAUDE_MD:-$HOME/.claude/CLAUDE.md}"
BASELINE="${SKILL_DRIFT_BASELINE:-$HOME/.agent/skill-drift.baseline}"
STALE_DAYS="${SKILL_STALE_DAYS:-180}"
USAGE_DAYS="${SKILL_USAGE_DAYS:-90}"
TOGGLE_DAYS="${SKILL_TOGGLE_DAYS:-30}"
# A listed skill used in this many sessions or fewer in TOGGLE_DAYS goes name-only, and a name-only
# one is listed again past twice it. One audit session reading a SKILL.md is a use, so a bar of 0
# brought 19 of 35 name-only skills back on 2026-11-08 (about 4.5K chars on every request) for one
# to four sessions each.
TOGGLE_MAX="${SKILL_TOGGLE_MAX_SESSIONS:-5}"
SETTINGS="${SKILL_SETTINGS:-$HOME/.claude/settings.json}"
# Every sign a skill was used: a Skill call, a typed command, its body loaded, its SKILL.md
# read, a file under it run, its CLI run. Counting only the first two hid skills that
# sessions reach by a route or a queue brief.
USE_KINDS=skill,slash,body,read,script,mention
# The one usage counter, by path beside this script: a worktree run uses the worktree's copy.
# It honours CLAUDE_TRANSCRIPTS itself.
RECALL="${CLAUDE_RECALL:-$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$0")")/claude-recall}"
# The owner of metadata.replaces (the hand-typed forms a skill retires): rows, row check, match.
HAND_METHODS="$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$0")")/../../.claude/hooks/hand_methods.py"

# The known categories. A skill outside this set is a typo or a taxonomy that
# grew without anyone deciding to grow it; either way it should be a review
# conversation, not a silent new directory.
CATEGORIES=" authoring memory messaging monitoring ops reference research tickets workstreams "

# The per-skill description budget, in bytes. Measured 2026-11-02: the listing
# shares about 30 KB across 129 skills and commands, and with descriptions
# totalling 72 KB, 88 of them arrived as a bare name. 300 B each keeps the
# whole corpus inside the listing.
DESC_MAX=300

# What this tree may spend of that shared listing: about 30 KB, less about 6 KB of
# built-in and plugin entries the harness adds that no deployed tree can show (measured
# from a session's listing 2026-11-06). Counting the tree against the full 30 KB would
# stay green past the point where entries really go bare.
LISTING_MAX="${SKILL_LISTING_MAX:-24576}"

# The one heading every skill carries (the majority form when the standard was written).
HEADING='## Scope and triggers'

MODE=report LINT_DIR="" DOCS_JSON=0 PROFILE=full RATCHET_BASE=origin/main ALLOW_EMPTY=0
case "${1:-}" in
  --quiet)          MODE=quiet ;;
  --json)           MODE=json ;;
  --new)            MODE=new ;;
  --write-baseline) MODE=write_baseline ;;
  --lint)                MODE=lint ;;
  --write-lint-baseline) MODE=write_lint_baseline ;;
  --ratchet)             MODE=ratchet ;;
  --docs)           MODE=docs; [ "${2:-}" = --json ] && DOCS_JSON=1 ;;
  --dups)           MODE=dups ;;
  --overrides)      MODE=overrides; [ "${2:-}" = --json ] && DOCS_JSON=1 ;;
  -h|--help) sed -n '2,/^set -uo pipefail/{/^#/p}' "$0" | sed 's/^# \?//'; exit 0 ;;
esac

DUPS_CHECK=0 DUPS_FILE=""
if [ "$MODE" = dups ]; then
  shift
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      --json)  DOCS_JSON=1 ;;
      --check) DUPS_CHECK=1 ;;
      --file)  DUPS_FILE="${2:-}"; case "$DUPS_FILE" in ""|--*) DUPS_FILE="" ;; esac; [ -n "$DUPS_FILE" ] || { echo "skill-drift --dups --file needs a path" >&2; exit 2; }; shift ;;
      *) echo "skill-drift --dups: unknown argument $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
    shift
  done
  [ "$DUPS_CHECK" = 1 ] && [ -n "$DUPS_FILE" ] && { echo "skill-drift --dups: --check and --file do not combine" >&2; exit 2; }
fi

# The modes that work on one checkout take [DIR] [--profile P]; --ratchet also takes BASE.
case "$MODE" in lint|write_lint_baseline|ratchet)
  shift
  while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
    case "$1" in
      --profile) PROFILE="${2:-}"; shift; [ $# -eq 0 ] || shift ;;
      --allow-empty) ALLOW_EMPTY=1; shift ;;
      *) if [ -z "$LINT_DIR" ]; then LINT_DIR="$1"; else RATCHET_BASE="$1"; fi; shift ;;
    esac
  done
  LINT_DIR="${LINT_DIR:-$PWD}"
  # A typo here would run the wrong contract and report it clean.
  case "$PROFILE" in full|project) ;; *) echo "skill-drift: unknown profile '$PROFILE' (full|project)" >&2; exit 2 ;; esac
  [ "$ALLOW_EMPTY" = 0 ] || [ "$MODE" = lint ] || { echo "skill-drift: --allow-empty goes with --lint only" >&2; exit 2; }
esac

LINT_BASELINE="${SKILL_LINT_BASELINE:-$LINT_DIR/tests/skill-lint.baseline}"

FINDINGS=()   # "TYPE|skill|item|detail"
add() { FINDINGS+=("$1|$2|$3|$4"); }

# Commands so ubiquitous that their absence means a broken box, not a stale
# skill. Flagging these would bury the one finding that matters.
COMMON=" cd ls cat echo grep sed awk cut sort uniq head tail find xargs tr wc
diff comm test printf read for while if then else fi do done case esac exit
export local return set shift source eval exec mkdir rm cp mv ln touch chmod
git curl jq ssh scp rsync tar gzip open pwd env sleep date true false npx bash
sh zsh python python3 node npm pnpm yarn make docker kubectl helm gh stow "

is_common() { case "$COMMON" in *" $1 "*) return 0 ;; *) return 1 ;; esac; }

# --- per-skill checks --------------------------------------------------------

# check_frontmatter <label> <file> <required-key>...
check_frontmatter() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" item key; shift 2
  item="$(basename "$f")"
  head -1 "$f" | grep -q '^---$' || { add NOFRONTMATTER "$skill" "$item" "no YAML frontmatter"; return 0; }
  for key in "$@"; do
    sed -n '2,/^---$/p' "$f" | grep -q "^$key:" || add NOFRONTMATTER "$skill" "$item" "frontmatter missing $key:"
  done
  return 0
}

# Frontmatter that satisfies every grep above can still be invalid YAML. The
# usual culprit is an unquoted description containing ": " (colon-space), which
# YAML reads as a nested mapping key. Claude Code then drops EVERY field: the
# name falls back to the directory, the description to the body's first line,
# and allowed-tools/model/disable-model-invocation silently stop applying.
# Nothing warns at any verbosity - which is how 19 skills sat broken while this
# lint reported them valid. Grep cannot answer "is this YAML"; only a parser can.
#
# An unavailable parser is a FINDING, not a skip. A check that quietly passes
# when it could not run is the failure mode this whole file is written against.
check_yaml() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" item out
  item="$(basename "$f")"
  out=$(fm "$f" | python3 -c '
import sys
try:
    import yaml
except ImportError:
    print("NOPARSER"); sys.exit(0)
# fm() emits the closing --- delimiter too; left in, YAML sees a second
# document and every skill fails identically.
lines = sys.stdin.read().split("\n")
while lines and not lines[-1].strip():
    lines.pop()
if lines and lines[-1].strip() == "---":
    lines.pop()
try:
    d = yaml.safe_load("\n".join(lines))
except Exception as e:
    print(" ".join(str(e).split())[:90]); sys.exit(0)
if not isinstance(d, dict):
    print("frontmatter is not a mapping")
' 2>/dev/null) || { add BADYAML "$skill" "$item" "could not run the YAML parser"; return 0; }
  [ -n "$out" ] || return 0
  if [ "$out" = NOPARSER ]; then
    add BADYAML "$skill" "$item" "PyYAML unavailable - frontmatter left unverified"
  else
    add BADYAML "$skill" "$item" "$out"
  fi
  return 0
}

# --- static checks (also run in CI, where nothing else can) -------------------

fm() { sed -n '2,/^---$/p' "$1"; }

# The frontmatter `name` must be kebab-case AND equal the directory name. Claude
# Code takes the command from the DIRECTORY for a personal skill and from `name`
# for a plugin skill, so a mismatch means the two disagree about what this skill
# is called - and which one wins depends on how it was installed.
check_name() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" name
  name=$(fm "$f" | grep -m1 '^name:' | sed 's/^name:[[:space:]]*//; s/[[:space:]]*$//')
  [ -n "$name" ] || return 0
  printf '%s' "$name" | grep -qE '^[a-z][a-z0-9]*(-[a-z0-9]+)*$' \
    || add BADNAME "$skill" "$name" "name is not kebab-case"
  [ "$name" = "$skill" ] \
    || add BADNAME "$skill" "$name" "frontmatter name does not match the directory"
  return 0
}

# category/tags/reviewed. `reviewed` is the one that decays: it records when the
# skill's claims were last checked against reality, which is the question git
# cannot answer.
check_meta() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" parent="$3" cat tags rev
  cat=$(fm "$f"  | sed -n 's/^  category:[[:space:]]*//p' | head -1 | tr -d ' ')
  tags=$(fm "$f" | sed -n 's/^  tags:[[:space:]]*//p'     | head -1)
  rev=$(fm "$f"  | sed -n 's/^  reviewed:[[:space:]]*//p' | head -1 | tr -d ' "')

  if [ -z "$cat" ]; then
    add BADMETA "$skill" category "missing metadata.category"
  else
    case "$CATEGORIES" in
      *" $cat "*) : ;;
      *) add BADMETA "$skill" "$cat" "category is not one of:$CATEGORIES" ;;
    esac
    # The directory IS the category. Two places to read it means one can be wrong.
    [ -z "$parent" ] || [ "$cat" = "$parent" ] \
      || add BADMETA "$skill" "$cat" "category disagrees with the parent directory ($parent)"
  fi

  [ -n "$tags" ] || add BADMETA "$skill" tags "missing metadata.tags"
  case "$tags" in
    ""|\[*\]) : ;;
    *) add BADMETA "$skill" tags "tags must be an inline list, e.g. [a, b]" ;;
  esac

  if [ -z "$rev" ]; then
    add BADMETA "$skill" reviewed "missing metadata.reviewed"
  elif ! printf '%s' "$rev" | grep -qE '^[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$'; then
    add BADMETA "$skill" "$rev" "reviewed must be a quoted YYYY-MM-DD date"
  fi
  return 0
}

# description + when_to_use are what the skill listing shows, so that is what is
# measured, and measured the way the consumer reads it: parsed YAML, so a folded
# `>-` block counts its folded value, not its indentation and line breaks. The
# old awk counter read raw lines and so over- or under-counted every block.
desc_bytes() {
  fm "$1" | python3 -c '
import sys
try:
    import yaml
except ImportError:
    sys.exit(0)
lines = sys.stdin.read().split("\n")
while lines and not lines[-1].strip():
    lines.pop()
if lines and lines[-1].strip() == "---":
    lines.pop()
try:
    d = yaml.safe_load("\n".join(lines))
except Exception:
    sys.exit(0)
if isinstance(d, dict):
    v = "".join(str(d.get(k) or "") for k in ("description", "when_to_use"))
    print(len(v.encode("utf-8")))
' 2>/dev/null
}

check_desc_len() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" n
  n=$(desc_bytes "$f")
  # No number means the frontmatter did not parse; check_yaml already reports that.
  [ -n "$n" ] || return 0
  [ "$n" -gt "$DESC_MAX" ] \
    && add LONGDESC "$skill" "${n}B" "description over ${DESC_MAX} bytes - it spends the listing budget other skills need"
  return 0
}

# The house plain-ASCII rule (see the Writing Style section of CLAUDE.md). One
# finding per skill, not per character, so the baseline stays stable while a
# skill is being cleaned up and disappears entirely once it is clean.
check_ascii() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" n
  n=$(grep -c -P '[\x{2014}\x{2013}\x{2192}\x{2190}\x{2265}\x{2264}\x{2026}\x{00A7}\x{00B7}]' "$f" 2>/dev/null || true)
  [ "${n:-0}" -gt 0 ] && add NONASCII "$skill" "$(basename "$f")" "$n line(s) with non-ASCII punctuation (em dash, arrow, ellipsis...)"
  return 0
}

# Claude Code (2.1.325) runs /\$(\d+)(?!\w)/ over the body after the frontmatter and
# swaps in argument N when it exists; `${1}` and `$(1)` do not match, so code keeps
# working and the finding names the lines.
check_argsub() {
  local lines
  lines=$(awk 'NR==1 && /^---$/ {fm=1; next} fm && /^---$/ {fm=0; next} !fm {print NR": "$0}' "$2" \
    | grep -P '\$\d+(?!\w)' | cut -d: -f1 | paste -sd, -)
  [ -n "$lines" ] && add ARGSUB "$1" "SKILL.md:$lines" "\$N in the body becomes argument N when the skill is called with arguments"
  return 0
}

check_heading() {
  grep -qE "^${HEADING}[[:space:]]*$" "$2" || add NOHEADING "$1" SKILL.md "no '$HEADING' heading"
  return 0
}

# Pull every ~/... or $HOME/... token out of the prose and test it. A path is
# only CHECKABLE if it is concrete: placeholders (<repo>, {project}, *.md, ...)
# describe a shape, not a file, and testing them would produce pure noise.
#
# The token keeps its < > { } * ? so the placeholder test below can see them:
# `~/.cache/x/p<N>/` cut at the < read as a missing `~/.cache/x/p`; now it is
# checked as `~/.cache/x`.
#
# A trailing - or . means the regex stopped mid-template (`.../briefs/YYYY-MM-DD-`,
# `.../2026-04-30-<slug>`), not that a file is missing. Capitalised sanitisation
# stand-ins (AcmeCorp, YYYY) are the same story as *.example.internal.
check_paths() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" p orig
  while IFS= read -r p; do
    [ -n "$p" ] || continue
    orig="$p"
    case "$p" in
      *'...'*) continue ;;
      # A placeholder or brace list: only the concrete directory before it is
      # checkable (`~/.openclaw/{a,b}` names a dead ~/.openclaw).
      *'<'*|*'>'*|*'{'*|*'}'*|*'*'*|*'?'*)
        p="${p%%[\<\>\{\}\*\?]*}"; p="${p%/*}"
        case "$p" in '~'|'$HOME'|'') continue ;; esac ;;
      *'example.internal'*|*AcmeCorp*|*YYYY*|*vX.Y.Z*) continue ;;
      *-|*.) continue ;;
      *'$'*) case "$p" in '$HOME/'*) : ;; *) continue ;; esac ;;
    esac
    p="${p/#\~\//$HOME/}"
    p="${p/#\$HOME\//$HOME/}"
    p="${p%/}"
    [ -e "$p" ] || add DEADPATH "$skill" "${p/#$HOME/\~}" "path does not exist$(at_line "$f" "$orig")"
  done < <(
    grep -v 'skill-drift: ignore' "$f" \
      | grep -oE '(~|\$HOME)/[A-Za-z0-9._~/<>{}*?-]*[A-Za-z0-9._>}*?-]' \
      | sort -u
  )
  return 0
}

# In --docs mode a finding carries the doc's first line naming it, since a doc
# has no SKILL.md-sized scope and the fixer needs to land on it. Off for skills:
# the detail string is part of the baseline key, so adding it would mark every
# baselined skill finding NEW.
at_line() {
  [ "$MODE" = docs ] || return 0
  local n
  n=$(grep -m1 -nF -- "$2" "$1" | cut -d: -f1)
  [ -n "$n" ] && printf ' (line %s)' "$n"
  return 0
}

# The first token of a COMMAND line inside a ```bash fence. Two filters keep this
# from reading prose as shell, which is the whole difficulty: a continuation line
# (previous line ended in \ | && ||) has no command in first position, and a line
# with no flag, path, assignment, pipe or quote in it is English, not a command.
# Without those, `bump the version` reports a missing tool called "bump".
check_cmds() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" c defined
  # A shell function a shell fence of the doc defines (`mem0() {`, `function mem0 {`) is
  # called by the steps after it, not looked up on PATH.
  defined=$(awk '
      /^[[:space:]]*```(bash|sh|shell|console)[[:space:]]*$/ { inblock=1; next }
      /^[[:space:]]*```/ { inblock=0; next }
      inblock && match($0, /^[[:space:]]*(function[[:space:]]+[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*|[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*[[:space:]]*\(\))/) {
        d = substr($0, RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/^[[:space:]]*(function[[:space:]]+)?/, "", d); sub(/[[:space:]]*\(\)$/, "", d); print d
      }' "$f")
  while IFS= read -r c; do
    [ -n "$c" ] || continue
    is_common "$c" && continue
    grep -qxF -- "$c" <<<"$defined" && continue
    command -v "$c" >/dev/null 2>&1 && continue
    add NOCMD "$skill" "$c" "command not found on PATH$(at_line "$f" "$c")"
  done < <(
    awk '
      /^[[:space:]]*```(bash|sh|shell|console)[[:space:]]*$/ { inblock=1; cont=0; inq=0; hd=""; next }
      /^[[:space:]]*```/ { inblock=0; next }
      !inblock || /skill-drift: ignore/ { next }
      {
        line = $0
        sub(/^[[:space:]]+/, "", line)
        # Data, not commands: a heredoc body, and the lines of a single-quoted
        # string that spans lines (a GraphQL or jq program). Their first words
        # ("number", "from") are not tools.
        if (hd != "") { if (line == hd) hd = ""; next }
        # Count the quotes that are not inside "..." or a trailing # comment, so
        # an apostrophe in a "..." string or a trailing comment opens no string.
        tmp = line; gsub(/"[^"]*"/, "", tmp); sub(/(^|[[:space:]])#.*$/, "", tmp)
        nq = gsub(/\047/, "", tmp)
        if (inq) { if (nq % 2) inq = 0; next }
        if (line ~ /^#/) next                   # a comment, not a root prompt
        if (nq % 2) inq = 1
        if (match(tmp, /<<-?[[:space:]]*["\047]?[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*/)) {
          hd = substr(tmp, RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/^<<-?[[:space:]]*["\047]?/, "", hd)
        }
        sub(/^[$][[:space:]]+/, "", line)
        was_cont = cont
        cont = (line ~ /(\\|\||&&|\|\|)[[:space:]]*$/)
        if (was_cont) next                      # continuation: no command here
        # A command line carries a flag, a path, a pipe/redirect, or is a bare
        # invocation. Prose ("bump the version", "crash-free rate > 99.5%")
        # carries none of those. Precision over recall: a noisy report is an
        # ignored report, and DEADPATH already does the heavy lifting.
        if (line ~ /(^| )--?[A-Za-z]/)      { print line; next }   # flag
        if (line ~ /(^| )[~\/]|[a-z]\//)    { print line; next }   # path
        if (line ~ /\||>|\$\(/)             { print line; next }   # pipe/subshell
        if (line ~ /^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*$/)    { print line; next }   # bare invocation
      }
    ' "$f" \
      | awk '{print $1}' \
      | grep -xE '[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*' \
      | sort -u
  )
  return 0
}

# --- registration checks -----------------------------------------------------

# A skill dir in either repo with no link in ~/.claude/skills is documented and
# unreachable - the agent cannot invoke it however well it is described.
# Discovery is by SKILL.md at any depth, matching skill-deploy, so this keeps
# working across the flat -> <category>/<name>/ move. Globbing */ instead would
# have "found" nine categories and reported all 60 real skills as undeployed.
find_skills() {
  local root="$1"
  [ -d "$root" ] || return 0
  find "$root" -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 3 -name SKILL.md -type f 2>/dev/null | sort
}

check_undeployed() {
  local repo="$1" name="$2" m skill
  [ -d "$repo/.claude/skills" ] || return 0
  while IFS= read -r m; do
    [ -n "$m" ] || continue
    skill="$(basename "$(dirname "$m")")"
    [ -e "$SKILLS/$skill" ] || add UNDEPLOYED "$skill" "$name" "in the $name repo but not linked into ~/.claude/skills"
  done < <(find_skills "$repo/.claude/skills")
  return 0
}

# reviewed is a claim about REALITY, and reality moves. Past STALE_DAYS the claim
# is not false, it is unsupported - nobody has looked.
check_stale() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" rev age
  rev=$(fm "$f" | sed -n 's/^  reviewed:[[:space:]]*//p' | head -1 | tr -d ' "')
  [ -n "$rev" ] || return 0
  age=$(( ( $(date +%s) - $(date -d "$rev" +%s 2>/dev/null || echo 0) ) / 86400 ))
  [ "$age" -gt "$STALE_DAYS" ] && add STALE "$skill" "$rev" "${age}d since last verified (limit ${STALE_DAYS}d)"
  return 0
}

# Every skill's description sits in the context window of every session whether
# it fires or not, so a skill nothing calls is a standing cost. Counted by
# `claude-recall stats` over USE_KINDS, so a skill the human reaches only by its slash
# command (captain, one-shot) or a session only by reading it is not reported unused.
#
# A SIGNAL, not a verdict. A release runbook is supposed to look idle between
# releases; what this is for is finding the ones that are idle because the router
# never picks them, which is the overlap problem skill-refine has had no data for.
declare -A USED=() BYPASS=() BYPASS_COVERED=() DENIED=() REPLACES=()
# The window start and the time every transcript root reaches back to, from the last load_usage.
USAGE_SINCE="" USAGE_COVERED=""
# load_usage [days] [bypass]: every skill with a use in the window (default USAGE_DAYS) into USED,
# as the number of sessions that used it by any of USE_KINDS. With "bypass" the same scan fills
# BYPASS (sessions with a call matching the skill's metadata.replaces) and BYPASS_COVERED (those
# of them that used the skill too), and DENIED (sessions a hook refused such a call in).
load_usage() {
  local s n c kinds="$USE_KINDS" json
  [ "${2:-}" = bypass ] && kinds="$kinds,bypass,denied"
  json="$("$RECALL" stats --days "${1:-$USAGE_DAYS}" --kind "$kinds" --top 0 --json 2>/dev/null)"
  # "|", not a tab: whitespace IFS would collapse an empty since into covered_from's slot.
  IFS='|' read -r USAGE_SINCE USAGE_COVERED < <(jq -r '"\(.since // "")|\(.covered_from // "")"' <<<"$json" 2>/dev/null)
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r s n; do
    [ -n "$s" ] && USED[$s]=$n
  done < <(jq -r '.skill_sessions // {} | to_entries[] | select(.value > 0) | "\(.key)\t\(.value)"' <<<"$json" 2>/dev/null)
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r s n c; do
    [ -n "$s" ] && BYPASS[$s]=$n BYPASS_COVERED[$s]=$c
  done < <(jq -r '.rows[]? | select(.kind == "bypass") | "\(.key)\t\(.sessions)\t\(.covered_sessions // 0)"' <<<"$json" 2>/dev/null)
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r s n; do
    [ -n "$s" ] && DENIED[$s]=$n
  done < <(jq -r '.rows[]? | select(.kind == "denied") | "\(.key)\t\(.sessions)"' <<<"$json" 2>/dev/null)
  return 0
}

# load_replaces: the deployed skills that declare metadata.replaces, into REPLACES (row count).
# hand_methods.py owns the rows; this only asks it.
load_replaces() {
  local id
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r id _; do
    [ -n "$id" ] && REPLACES[${id%#*}]=$(( ${REPLACES[${id%#*}]:-0} + 1 ))
  done < <(CLAUDE_SKILLS="$SKILLS" python3 "$HAND_METHODS" --list 2>/dev/null)
  return 0
}

# A skill that declares the hand forms it replaces, with no use and no hand-typed form in the
# window, ran or denied, is RETIRE: nobody does that work at all. That says more than UNUSED, so
# it replaces it. A denied form is demand the guard redirected, never a reason to retire.
check_unused() {
  local skill="$1"
  # No transcripts at all means we cannot tell, which is not the same as unused.
  [ ${#USED[@]} -gt 0 ] || return 0
  [ "${USED[$skill]:-0}" -gt 0 ] && return 0
  if [ -n "${REPLACES[$skill]:-}" ] && [ "${BYPASS[$skill]:-0}" -eq 0 ] && [ "${DENIED[$skill]:-0}" -eq 0 ]; then
    add RETIRE "$skill" "${USAGE_DAYS}d" "no use and no hand-typed form of its work in the last ${USAGE_DAYS}d"
  else
    add UNUSED "$skill" "${USAGE_DAYS}d" "no use in the last ${USAGE_DAYS}d of transcripts"
  fi
  return 0
}

# BYPASSED: sessions typed a form the skill's metadata.replaces names and never used the skill.
check_bypass() {
  local skill="$1" n="${BYPASS[$1]:-0}" cov="${BYPASS_COVERED[$1]:-0}"
  [ $(( n - cov )) -gt 0 ] || return 0
  add BYPASSED "$skill" "${USAGE_DAYS}d" "$(( n - cov )) of $n sessions typed a form its metadata.replaces names, without the skill"
  return 0
}

# metadata.replaces rows the guard would skip are a lint finding here, not a silent no-op there.
check_replaces() {
  local skill="$1" f="$2" line
  grep -q '^  replaces:' "$f" || return 0
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -n "$line" ] && add BADMETA "$skill" replaces "${line//|//}"
  done < <(python3 "$HAND_METHODS" --check "$f" 2>&1)
  return 0
}

check_registration() {
  local skill
  [ -f "$CLAUDE_MD" ] || { add DEADPATH CLAUDE.md "${CLAUDE_MD/#$HOME/\~}" "CLAUDE.md not found"; return 0; }

  # Bulleted in CLAUDE.md but absent from disk: the agent will try to invoke it.
  #
  # The "Retired" block is excluded, because naming a dead skill there is the
  # entire point of it - it is what stops the agent reaching for a skill that
  # used to exist. Flagging those would mean the only way to satisfy the check
  # is to delete the warning, which is backwards.
  while IFS= read -r skill; do
    [ -n "$skill" ] || continue
    [ -e "$SKILLS/$skill" ] || add GHOST "$skill" CLAUDE.md "bulleted in CLAUDE.md but not on disk"
  done < <(
    awk '
      /^\*\*Retired/        { skip=1; next }
      /^\*\*/               { skip=0 }
      /^##/                 { skip=0 }
      !skip
    ' "$CLAUDE_MD" \
      | grep -oE '^- `[a-z][a-z0-9:-]*`' | sed 's/^- `//; s/`$//' | grep -v ':' | sort -u
  )
  return 0
}

# The whole listing, measured the way it arrives: one `- name: description` line per
# skill and command that is listed. Over the deployed tree because only that holds both
# repos. A `paths:` or `disable-model-invocation: true` entry costs nothing until used,
# and a skill the toggle keeps "name-only" in SETTINGS costs its name line, not its description.
check_listing() {
  local total=0 f name n
  local -A nameonly=()
  if [ -f "$SETTINGS" ]; then
    while IFS= read -r name; do [ -n "$name" ] && nameonly[$name]=1; done \
      < <(jq -r '.skillOverrides // {} | to_entries[] | select(.value == "name-only") | .key' "$SETTINGS" 2>/dev/null)
  fi
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r name f; do
    fm "$f" | grep -E '^paths:|^disable-model-invocation:[[:space:]]*true' >/dev/null && continue
    n=0
    [ -z "${nameonly[$name]:-}" ] && n=$(desc_bytes "$f")
    total=$((total + ${#name} + 5 + ${n:-0}))
  done < <(
    for f in "$SKILLS"/*/SKILL.md; do
      [ -e "$f" ] && printf '%s\t%s\n' "$(basename "$(dirname "$f")")" "$f"
    done
    [ -d "$COMMANDS" ] && find -L "$COMMANDS" -name '*.md' -type f | while IFS= read -r f; do
      name="${f#"$COMMANDS"/}"; name="${name%.md}"
      printf '%s\t%s\n' "${name//\//:}" "$f"
    done
  )
  [ "$total" -gt "$LISTING_MAX" ] \
    && add LISTBUDGET listing "${total}B" "listed skills and commands are over the ${LISTING_MAX}-byte budget, so the last ones arrive as a bare name"
  return 0
}

# --- run ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- static pass, shared by --lint and the full local report ------------------
#
# Runs over SOURCE trees (a repo checkout), because that is all CI has and
# because the parent directory - which check_meta compares the category against -
# only exists there. The deployed tree is flat by design.
# SEEN_NAME is scoped to ONE namespace per call, because uniqueness is a property
# of the namespace and not of the filesystem. ~/.claude/skills is a single flat
# tree fed by both dotfiles repos, so a name repeated across them is ambiguous.
declare -A SEEN_NAME=()
# run_static <contract> <root>...
#
# <contract> is `full` or `project`.
#
#   full        the Claude corpus: category/tags/reviewed, the category directory
#               layout, the description budget, the house ASCII rule, the heading.
#   project     the same minus category/tags/reviewed and the category layout, which
#               mean nothing outside dotfiles. --profile project: a game repo's flat
#               .claude/skills/<name>/ is still a Claude skill, listed like any other.
#
# Codex reads this same corpus: .config/codex/sync-codex-home.sh links each deployed
# skill into ~/.codex/skills, so there is no second corpus to lint.
run_static() {
  local contract="$1"; shift
  local root m d s parent c
  SEEN_NAME=()
  for root in "$@"; do
    [ -d "$root" ] || continue
    while IFS= read -r m; do
      [ -n "$m" ] || continue
      d="$(dirname "$m")"; s="$(basename "$d")"
      parent="$(basename "$(dirname "$d")")"
      # A flat skill sits directly under skills/, so it has no category dir.
      [ "$parent" = "skills" ] && parent=""
      if [ -n "${SEEN_NAME[$s]:-}" ]; then
        add DUPNAME "$s" "$d" "also defined at ${SEEN_NAME[$s]}"
      else
        SEEN_NAME[$s]="$d"
      fi
      check_frontmatter "$s" "$m" name description
      check_yaml        "$s" "$m"
      check_name        "$s" "$m"
      [ "$contract" = full ] && check_meta "$s" "$m" "$parent"
      check_desc_len "$s" "$m"
      check_ascii    "$s" "$m"
      check_heading  "$s" "$m"
      check_argsub   "$s" "$m"
      check_replaces "$s" "$m"
    done < <(find_skills "$root")
  done
  # A category directory that shares a name with a skill: comms/comms/ makes
  # `git mv` refuse, and it silently swallowed two sibling skills once already.
  #
  # A directory is a CATEGORY only if it does not itself hold a SKILL.md. Without
  # that test every skill in the flat layout counts as a category colliding with
  # itself, which reported 68 findings for a corpus that had none.
  [ "$contract" = full ] || return 0
  for root in "$@"; do
    [ -d "$root" ] || continue
    for d in "$root"/*/; do
      [ -d "$d" ] || continue
      [ -f "$d/SKILL.md" ] && continue
      c="$(basename "$d")"
      [ -n "${SEEN_NAME[$c]:-}" ] && add CATCOLLIDE "$c" "$root/$c" "a category cannot share a name with a skill"
    done
  done
  return 0
}

find_commands() { [ -d "$1/.claude/commands" ] && find "$1/.claude/commands" -name '*.md' -type f | sort; }
find_agents()   { [ -d "$1/.claude/agents" ] && find "$1/.claude/agents" -maxdepth 1 -name '*.md' -type f | sort; }

# check_entry <label> <file> <required-key>...
# A file with no frontmatter stops at that finding: fm would hand its prose to the parser.
check_entry() {
  local label="$1" f="$2"
  check_frontmatter "$@"
  check_ascii "$label" "$f"
  head -1 "$f" | grep -q '^---$' || return 0
  check_yaml     "$label" "$f"
  check_desc_len "$label" "$f"
}

# run_entries <repo>...: commands and agents share the listing and the house rules with
# skills. A command's name is its path, so only an agent has a name: key to require.
run_entries() {
  local repo m rel
  for repo in "$@"; do
    while IFS= read -r m; do
      [ -n "$m" ] || continue
      rel="${m#"$repo/.claude/commands/"}"
      check_entry "command:${rel%.md}" "$m" description
    done < <(find_commands "$repo")
    while IFS= read -r m; do
      [ -n "$m" ] || continue
      check_entry "agent:$(basename "$m" .md)" "$m" name description
    done < <(find_agents "$repo")
  done
}

# The global doc set: what loads into, or is pointed at by, every session.
# Memory dirs exist once per config dir (~/.claude, ~/.claude-personal, ...) but
# resolve to one tree, so they are deduped by real path.
doc_set() {
  # readlink -e drops missing paths and unmatched globs; -f below drops directories.
  if [ -n "${DRIFT_DOCS:-}" ]; then printf '%s\n' "$DRIFT_DOCS"
  else printf '%s\n' "$CLAUDE_MD" "$PUB/CLAUDE.md" "$PRIV/AGENTS.md" \
    "$HOME"/.local/src/docs/*.md "$HOME"/.config/agentctl/*.md \
    "$HOME"/.agent/anchors/*.md "$HOME"/.agent/lessons/*.md \
    "$HOME"/.claude*/projects/*/memory/*.md
  fi | xargs -d '\n' -r readlink -e -- | sort -u | while IFS= read -r f; do [ -f "$f" ] && printf '%s\n' "$f"; done
}

# --- --overrides: the skill-usage toggle --------------------------------------
#
# A route (another skill, a global doc or a runner conf naming the skill) is how a reader gets to it, so
# whether a routed skill is listed stays the human's call: the count never hides one. The name
# matches whole (no letter, digit, - or _ beside it). A one-word name like scope matches prose
# too, which errs on the side that leaves a skill alone.
routed() {
  grep -lE "(^|[^[:alnum:]_-])$1([^[:alnum:]_-]|$)" "${ROUTE_DOCS[@]}" 2>/dev/null |
    grep -qvxF "$SKILLS/$1/SKILL.md"
}

if [ "$MODE" = overrides ]; then
  [ -f "$SETTINGS" ] || { echo "skill-drift --overrides: no settings file $SETTINGS" >&2; exit 2; }
  cur=$(jq -c '.skillOverrides // {}' "$SETTINGS") || { echo "skill-drift --overrides: $SETTINGS does not parse" >&2; exit 2; }
  load_usage "$TOGGLE_DAYS"
  [ ${#USED[@]} -gt 0 ] || { echo "skill-drift --overrides: no use counted in ${TOGGLE_DAYS}d of transcripts, which is not the same as nothing used" >&2; exit 2; }
  # Both are UTC in one format, so they compare as strings.
  if [ -z "$USAGE_SINCE" ] || [ -z "$USAGE_COVERED" ] || [[ "$USAGE_COVERED" > "$USAGE_SINCE" ]]; then
    echo "skill-drift --overrides: the transcripts are covered from ${USAGE_COVERED:-nothing}, not the ${TOGGLE_DAYS}d window from ${USAGE_SINCE:-an unknown start}, so a skill used before then would read as unused" >&2
    exit 3
  fi
  # Where a reader or a runner is sent to a skill. Not lessons, anchors or memory: they are history, and a skill named in prose there is not routed.
  AC="$HOME/.config/agentctl"
  mapfile -t ROUTE_DOCS < <(for f in "$CLAUDE_MD" "$PUB/CLAUDE.md" "$PRIV/AGENTS.md" "$SKILLS"/*/SKILL.md "$HOME"/.local/src/docs/*.md "$AC"/*.md "$AC"/triage-routes "$AC"/agents/*.conf "$AC"/roles/* "$AC"/prompts/*; do [ -f "$f" ] && printf '%s\n' "$f"; done)
  declare -A STATE=()
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r s v; do STATE[$s]=$v; done < <(jq -r 'to_entries[] | "\(.key)\t\(.value)"' <<<"$cur")
  # A paths-scoped or model-disabled skill has its own listing rule; the toggle leaves it alone.
  pinned() { [ -f "$1" ] && fm "$1" | grep -qE '^paths:|^disable-model-invocation:[[:space:]]*true'; }
  hide=() restore=()
  while IFS= read -r s; do
    f="$SKILLS/$s/SKILL.md" state=${STATE[$s]:-listed}
    n=${USED[$s]:-0}
    if [ "$n" -gt $((TOGGLE_MAX * 2)) ]; then
      [ "$state" = name-only ] && ! pinned "$f" && restore+=("$s")
    elif [ "$n" -le "$TOGGLE_MAX" ] && [ "$state" = listed ] && [ -f "$f" ] && ! pinned "$f" && ! routed "$s"; then
      hide+=("$s")
    fi
  done < <({ for f in "$SKILLS"/*/SKILL.md; do [ -e "$f" ] && basename "$(dirname "$f")"; done
             for s in "${!STATE[@]}"; do [ "${STATE[$s]}" = name-only ] && echo "$s"; done; } | sort -u)
  if [ "$DOCS_JSON" = 1 ]; then
    # Skill names are directory names with no spaces, so each list travels as one string.
    jq -cS --argjson d "$TOGGLE_DAYS" --argjson m "$TOGGLE_MAX" --arg h "${hide[*]}" --arg r "${restore[*]}" '
      ($h | split(" ") - [""]) as $h | ($r | split(" ") - [""]) as $r
      | {days: $d, max: $m, hide: $h, restore: $r, skillOverrides: (reduce $h[] as $s (del(.[$r[]]); .[$s] = "name-only"))}' <<<"$cur"
  else
    echo "skill-toggle over ${TOGGLE_DAYS}d, name-only at $TOGGLE_MAX sessions or fewer, listed past $((TOGGLE_MAX * 2)): hide ${#hide[@]} [${hide[*]}], restore ${#restore[@]} [${restore[*]}]"
  fi
  exit 0
fi

if [ "$MODE" = docs ]; then
  ndocs=0
  while IFS= read -r f; do
    ndocs=$((ndocs + 1))
    label="${f/#$HOME/\~}"
    check_paths "$label" "$f"
    check_cmds  "$label" "$f"
  done < <(doc_set)
  # A run that scanned nothing is a failure, never a clean report.
  [ "$ndocs" -gt 0 ] || { echo "skill-drift --docs: no docs found - refusing to report success" >&2; exit 2; }
  n=${#FINDINGS[@]}
  if [ "$DOCS_JSON" = 1 ]; then
    printf '%s\n' ${FINDINGS[@]+"${FINDINGS[@]}"} | jq -Rnc --argjson docs "$ndocs" \
      '[inputs | select(. != "") | split("|") | {type: .[0], doc: .[1], item: .[2], detail: (.[3:] | join("|"))}]
       | {docs: $docs, drift: length, findings: .}'
  else
    for f in ${FINDINGS[@]+"${FINDINGS[@]}"}; do
      IFS='|' read -r t s p d <<< "$f"
      printf '  %-9s %-60s %-50s %s\n' "$t" "$s" "$p" "$d"
    done
    printf 'skill-drift --docs: %s finding(s) across %s doc(s)\n' "$n" "$ndocs"
  fi
  [ "$n" -eq 0 ] && exit 0 || exit 1
fi

# --- dups: the same instruction written in two homes ---------------------------
#
# The corpus is doc_set plus the skills, roles and prompts that also carry rules. A pair is
# two lines in different files, so a file restating itself never counts. Kinds, globs and
# ranks come from instruction-homes.json in the agentctl conf (private repo); the global file
# is whatever $CLAUDE_MD resolves to, as everywhere else in this script.
AGENTCTL_DIR="${AGENTCTL_CONF:-$HOME/.config/agentctl}"
dup_corpus() {
  local conf="$AGENTCTL_DIR"
  { doc_set
    { find -L "$SKILLS" -maxdepth 3 -name SKILL.md
      find -L "$conf/roles" -maxdepth 1 -name '*.role'
      find -L "$conf/prompts" -maxdepth 1 -name '*.md'
    } 2>/dev/null | xargs -d '\n' -r readlink -e --
  } | sort -u
}

if [ "$MODE" = dups ]; then
  nfiles=$(dup_corpus | wc -l)
  [ "$nfiles" -gt 0 ] || { echo "skill-drift --dups: no docs found - refusing to report success" >&2; exit 2; }
  homes="$AGENTCTL_DIR/instruction-homes.json"
  [ -r "$homes" ] || { echo "skill-drift --dups: no $homes - no rank to pick a winner by" >&2; exit 2; }
  dups_max=""
  if [ "$DUPS_CHECK" = 1 ]; then
    thr="${KPI_THRESHOLDS:-$AGENTCTL_DIR/kpi-thresholds}"
    dups_max=$(sed -n 's/^dup_pairs_max=\([0-9][0-9]*\)$/\1/p' "$thr" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)
    [ -n "$dups_max" ] || { echo "skill-drift --dups --check: no dup_pairs_max=<n> in $thr - nothing to hold the count to" >&2; exit 2; }
  fi
  dups_only="$DUPS_FILE"; [ -z "$dups_only" ] || dups_only=$(readlink -m -- "$DUPS_FILE")
  dup_corpus | DUPS_MAX="$dups_max" DUPS_ONLY="$dups_only" DUPS_GLOBAL="$(readlink -e "$CLAUDE_MD" 2>/dev/null)" DUPS_HOMES="$homes" DUPS_JSON="$DOCS_JSON" DUPS_HOME="$HOME" python3 -c '
import fnmatch, json, os, re, sys
from collections import Counter, defaultdict

# Provisional, confidence low: revisit after two weeks of nightly counts (plan arch-instructions-one-home).
MIN_CHARS, MIN_TOKENS, N, THRESHOLD = 90, 5, 4, 0.3
HOME, GLOBAL = os.environ["DUPS_HOME"], os.environ.get("DUPS_GLOBAL", "")
try:
    KINDS = [(k["kind"], int(k["rank"]), list(k["globs"])) for k in json.load(open(os.environ["DUPS_HOMES"]))["kinds"]]
    RANK = dict((k, r) for k, r, _ in KINDS)
    assert KINDS and "global" in RANK
except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError, AssertionError) as e:
    print("skill-drift --dups: %s is not a homes file (kinds with kind, rank, globs; one named global): %r" % (os.environ["DUPS_HOMES"], e), file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)

def home(path):
    """(home, rank): the global file is $CLAUDE_MD; any other path takes the first kind whose glob matches."""
    if path == GLOBAL: return "global", RANK["global"]
    for kind, rank, globs in KINDS:
        if any(fnmatch.fnmatchcase(path, g) for g in globs): return kind, rank
    return "other", 0

nread = 0
lines = []   # (path, lineno, text, shingles)
for path in sys.stdin.read().split("\n"):
    if not path: continue
    skip = False
    try: raw = open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read().split("\n")
    except OSError: continue
    nread += 1
    for i, text in enumerate(raw, 1):
        text = text.strip()
        if text.startswith("<!-- AUTO:START"): skip = True; continue
        if text.startswith("<!-- AUTO:END"): skip = False; continue
        if skip or len(text) < MIN_CHARS: continue
        tok = re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", " ", text.lower()).split()
        if len(tok) < MIN_TOKENS: continue
        lines.append((path, i, text, {" ".join(tok[k:k + N]) for k in range(len(tok) - N + 1)}))

if nread == 0:
    print("skill-drift --dups: no readable docs - refusing to report success", file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(2)
index = defaultdict(list)
for n, (_, _, _, sh) in enumerate(lines):
    for g in sh: index[g].append(n)

ONLY, MAX = os.environ.get("DUPS_ONLY", ""), os.environ.get("DUPS_MAX", "")
pairs = []
for a, (pa, _, _, sa) in enumerate(lines):
    shared = Counter(b for g in sa for b in index[g] if b > a and lines[b][0] != pa)
    for b, c in shared.items():
        score = c / (len(sa) + len(lines[b][3]) - c)
        # Every anchor is one generated template, so two anchors always share its lines.
        if score >= THRESHOLD and not (home(pa)[0] == "anchor" == home(lines[b][0])[0]): pairs.append((score, a, b))
pairs.sort(key=lambda t: (-t[0], t[1], t[2]))

def side(n):
    path, lineno, text, _ = lines[n]
    h, r = home(path)
    return {"home": h, "rank": r, "file": ("~" + path[len(HOME):]) if path.startswith(HOME + "/") else path, "line": lineno, "text": text[:160]}

items = []
for score, a, b in pairs:
    x, y = side(a), side(b)
    win = "pick" if x["rank"] == y["rank"] else (x if x["rank"] > y["rank"] else y)["home"]
    items.append({"score": round(score, 2), "a": x, "b": y, "winner": win})
if ONLY: items = [i for i in items if ONLY in (os.path.realpath(i[s]["file"].replace("~", HOME, 1)) for s in "ab")]
involved = len({(i[s]["file"], i[s]["line"]) for i in items for s in "ab"})
by_home = Counter("/".join(sorted((i["a"]["home"], i["b"]["home"]))) for i in items)

over = max(len(items) - int(MAX), 0) if MAX else 0
if os.environ["DUPS_JSON"] == "1":
    out = {"files": nread, "pairs": len(items), "lines": involved,
           "threshold": THRESHOLD, "by_home": dict(by_home.most_common()), "items": items}
    if MAX: out.update(max=int(MAX), over=over)
    print(json.dumps(out))
else:
    for i in (items if not MAX or over else []):
        a, b = i["a"], i["b"]
        print("  %.2f  %s %s:%d  <->  %s %s:%d  winner: %s" % (i["score"], a["home"], a["file"], a["line"], b["home"], b["file"], b["line"], i["winner"]))
        print("        %s" % a["text"][:100])
        print("        %s" % b["text"][:100])
    for k, v in by_home.most_common(): print("  %3d  %s" % (v, k))
    print("skill-drift --dups: %d pair(s), %d line(s) across %d file(s)" % (len(items), involved, nread))
    if MAX and over: print("skill-drift --dups --check: %d over the baseline of %s (dup_pairs_max): remove the restated line, keep the winning copy" % (over, MAX))
    elif MAX and len(items) < int(MAX): print("skill-drift --dups --check: %d under the baseline: lower dup_pairs_max to %d" % (int(MAX) - len(items), len(items)))
sys.exit(1 if (over or (ONLY and items)) else 0)
'
  exit "${PIPESTATUS[1]}"
fi

# --- ratchet (what makes the lint baseline shrink-only) ------------------------
#
# Compares the file as it stands with the file at the merge base. A file missing there,
# for any reason, counts as empty, so every line is an added line: this fails closed.
if [ "$MODE" = ratchet ]; then
  mb=$(git -C "$LINT_DIR" merge-base "$RATCHET_BASE" HEAD 2>/dev/null) || {
    echo "skill-ratchet: no merge base with $RATCHET_BASE (shallow clone?) - refusing to report success" >&2
    exit 2
  }
  added=$(LC_ALL=C comm -13 \
    <(git -C "$LINT_DIR" show "$mb:tests/skill-lint.baseline" 2>/dev/null | LC_ALL=C sort -u) \
    <(LC_ALL=C sort -u "$LINT_BASELINE" 2>/dev/null))
  if [ -z "$added" ]; then
    echo "skill-ratchet: no baseline line added since ${mb:0:8}"
    exit 0
  fi
  raise=$(git -C "$LINT_DIR" log "$mb..HEAD" --format='%(trailers:key=Skill-Baseline-Raise,valueonly)' | grep -m1 .)
  sed 's/^/  ADDED  /' <<< "$added"
  if [ -n "$raise" ]; then
    printf 'skill-ratchet: %d line(s) added under Skill-Baseline-Raise: %s\n' "$(wc -l <<< "$added")" "$raise"
    exit 0
  fi
  printf '\nskill-ratchet: %d line(s) added since %s. Fix the finding instead. A new check freezing old debt says so with a commit trailer, Skill-Baseline-Raise: <why>.\n' "$(wc -l <<< "$added")" "${mb:0:8}"
  exit 1
fi

if [ "$MODE" = lint ] || [ "$MODE" = write_lint_baseline ]; then
  run_static "$PROFILE" "$LINT_DIR/.claude/skills"
  run_entries "$LINT_DIR"
else
  # One namespace: the dotfiles pair feed one flat ~/.claude/skills.
  run_static full "$PUB/.claude/skills" "$PRIV/.claude/skills"
  run_entries "$PUB" "$PRIV"

  load_usage "$USAGE_DAYS" bypass
  load_replaces
  for d in "$SKILLS"/*/; do
    [ -e "$d/SKILL.md" ] || continue
    s="$(basename "$d")"
    check_paths  "$s" "$d/SKILL.md"
    check_cmds   "$s" "$d/SKILL.md"
    check_stale  "$s" "$d/SKILL.md"
    check_unused "$s"
    check_bypass "$s"
  done

  check_undeployed "$PUB"  public
  check_undeployed "$PRIV" private
  check_registration
  check_listing
fi

# Sorting keeps the baseline file stable across runs, so `git diff` on it shows
# real change instead of reordering noise. Guarded on non-empty: `printf '%s\n'`
# with no arguments still prints ONE blank line, which turned a clean run into a
# single empty "finding" and made --quiet exit 1 while the report said clean.
if [ ${#FINDINGS[@]} -gt 0 ]; then
  mapfile -t FINDINGS < <(printf '%s\n' "${FINDINGS[@]}" | sort)
fi
n=${#FINDINGS[@]}

if [ "$MODE" = write_baseline ]; then
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$BASELINE")"
  [ "$n" -gt 0 ] && printf "%s\n" "${FINDINGS[@]}" > "$BASELINE" || : > "$BASELINE"
  echo "skill-drift: baselined $n finding(s) -> ${BASELINE/#$HOME/\~}"
  exit 0
fi

# --- lint (what CI runs) ------------------------------------------------------
#
# The baseline holds `TYPE|subject` lines. Only debt that predates its rule may sit there:
# NONASCII, NOHEADING, and anything on a command or agent, linted only since 2026-11.
# Every other check was clean when it landed, so it stays a hard error from the first run.
freezable() {
  case "$1|$2" in
    NONASCII\|*|NOHEADING\|*|*\|command:*|*\|agent:*) return 0 ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
}

if [ "$MODE" = write_lint_baseline ]; then
  mkdir -p "$(dirname "$LINT_BASELINE")"
  : > "$LINT_BASELINE"
  for f in ${FINDINGS[@]+"${FINDINGS[@]}"}; do
    IFS='|' read -r t s _ <<< "$f"
    freezable "$t" "$s" && printf '%s|%s\n' "$t" "$s" >> "$LINT_BASELINE"
  done
  LC_ALL=C sort -u -o "$LINT_BASELINE" "$LINT_BASELINE"
  echo "skill-lint: baselined $(wc -l < "$LINT_BASELINE") finding(s) -> $LINT_BASELINE"
  exit 0
fi

if [ "$MODE" = lint ]; then
  errs=0
  declare -A CURRENT=()
  for f in ${FINDINGS[@]+"${FINDINGS[@]}"}; do
    IFS='|' read -r t s p d <<< "$f"
    CURRENT["$t|$s"]=1
    if freezable "$t" "$s" && [ -f "$LINT_BASELINE" ] && grep -qxF "$t|$s" "$LINT_BASELINE"; then
      continue   # known debt, tracked in the baseline
    fi
    printf '  FAIL  %-13s %-24s %-24s %s\n' "$t" "$s" "$p" "$d"
    errs=$((errs + 1))
  done
  # A line whose debt is gone would let that debt back in unnoticed. A warning, not a
  # failure: the fix is one deleted line, and it should not hold up unrelated work.
  warn=""
  [ -z "${GITHUB_ACTIONS:-}" ] || warn="::warning::"
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -z "$line" ] || [ -n "${CURRENT[$line]:-}" ] && continue
    printf '%sskill-lint: stale baseline line, delete it: %s\n' "$warn" "$line"
  done < <(cat "$LINT_BASELINE" 2>/dev/null)
  scanned=$(find_skills "$LINT_DIR/.claude/skills" | wc -l)
  entries=$( { find_commands "$LINT_DIR"; find_agents "$LINT_DIR"; } | wc -l)
  # An empty input list is a FAILURE, not a clean run. Every tooling failure in
  # this repo has been a silent success, and a lint that scans nothing and says
  # "all good" is the purest form of it. --allow-empty is the caller saying so in
  # advance: a game repo whose only skills are generated and untracked.
  if [ "$((scanned + entries))" -eq 0 ]; then
    if [ "$ALLOW_EMPTY" = 1 ] && [ "$errs" -eq 0 ]; then
      echo "skill-lint: no skills, commands or agents under $LINT_DIR/.claude, as --allow-empty expects"
      exit 0
    fi
    echo "skill-lint: found NO skills, commands or agents under $LINT_DIR/.claude - refusing to report success"
    exit 2
  fi
  if [ "$errs" -gt 0 ]; then
    printf '\nskill-lint: %d error(s) across %d skill(s) and %d command or agent file(s). The standard: ~/.local/src/docs/skills.md\n' "$errs" "$scanned" "$entries"
    exit 1
  fi
  printf 'skill-lint: %d skill(s) valid, and %d command or agent file(s)\n' "$scanned" "$entries"
  exit 0
fi

# A YAML parser error quotes the text it choked on, and the nightly reads this as JSON.
# shellcheck source=../lib/fmt.sh
. "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")")/../lib/fmt.sh"

is_new() { [ -f "$BASELINE" ] || return 0; ! grep -qxF "$1" "$BASELINE"; }

NEW=()
for f in ${FINDINGS[@]+"${FINDINGS[@]}"}; do
  is_new "$f" && NEW+=("$f")
done
nnew=${#NEW[@]}

case "$MODE" in
  quiet) [ "$nnew" -eq 0 ] && exit 0 || exit 1 ;;
  json)
    printf '{"drift":%s,"new":%s,"findings":[' "$n" "$nnew"
    for i in "${!FINDINGS[@]}"; do
      IFS='|' read -r t s p d <<< "${FINDINGS[$i]}"
      [ "$i" -gt 0 ] && printf ','
      printf '{"type":"%s","skill":"%s","item":"%s","detail":"%s","new":%s}' \
        "$t" "$s" "$(json_esc "$p")" "$(json_esc "$d")" "$(is_new "${FINDINGS[$i]}" && echo true || echo false)"
    done
    printf ']}\n'
    [ "$nnew" -eq 0 ] && exit 0 || exit 1 ;;
esac

if [ "$MODE" = new ]; then
  if [ "$nnew" -eq 0 ]; then echo "skill-drift: no NEW drift since the baseline"; exit 0; fi
  printf 'skill-drift: %s NEW finding(s) since the baseline\n\n' "$nnew"
  for f in "${NEW[@]}"; do
    IFS='|' read -r t s p d <<< "$f"
    printf '  %-13s %-24s %-46s %s\n' "$t" "$s" "$p" "$d"
  done
  exit 1
fi

if [ "$n" -eq 0 ]; then
  echo "skill-drift: clean - every skill still describes a system that exists"
  exit 0
fi
printf 'skill-drift: %s finding(s) (%s new since the baseline)\n\n' "$n" "$nnew"
for f in "${FINDINGS[@]}"; do
  IFS='|' read -r t s p d <<< "$f"
  printf '  %-13s %-24s %-46s %s%s\n' "$t" "$s" "$p" "$d" "$(is_new "$f" && echo '  <-- NEW')"
done
cat <<'EOF'

  DEADPATH   -> the path moved. Find it (`fd`/`find` both repos) and fix the SKILL.md
  NOCMD      -> the tool is not on PATH. Name its real location, or drop the step
  UNDEPLOYED -> link them all:  skill-deploy    (--dry-run to preview)
  GHOST      -> the skill is retired; remove the bullet from CLAUDE.md
  BADNAME    -> rename the directory or the frontmatter so the two agree
  DUPNAME    -> one flat namespace across both repos; rename one of them
  BADMETA    -> fix the metadata block (see ~/.local/src/docs/skills.md)
  BADYAML    -> use a `description: >-` block; a bare `: ` in the value breaks the parse
  CATCOLLIDE -> rename the category; it cannot share a name with a skill
  LONGDESC   -> cut the description to a trigger line (what it is, the main
                "use when" phrases, one "not this, use X"); detail goes in the body
  NONASCII   -> plain ASCII (see the Writing Style rules)
  ARGSUB     -> write `${1}` in shell and `$(1)` in awk; money in words ("5 dollars")
  NOHEADING  -> say what the skill covers and when under `## Scope and triggers`
                (`--write-lint-baseline` freezes debt in these two, it does not make
                the rule optional, and `--ratchet` refuses a line added to it)
  STALE      -> re-verify the skill against reality, THEN bump metadata.reviewed.
                Bumping the date without checking is worse than leaving it stale.
  UNUSED     -> a signal, not a verdict. A release runbook is meant to look idle;
                a skill the router never picks is the one to look at
  RETIRE     -> no use and no hand-typed form of its work: propose retiring it with
                `/skill-refine`; the human says yes before anything is deleted
  BYPASSED   -> sessions did the skill's work by hand (`claude-recall stats --kind bypass`
                shows one). A deny row: widen its match. A report row: script the form
                and make the row deny, or fix the step that sends sessions around it
  LISTBUDGET -> shorten descriptions, or give a domain skill `paths:` so it is listed
                only where it applies

  Reason about these with `/skill-refine audit` - it adds the lessons/mem0 layer
  and writes proposed diffs. Do NOT silence a finding by deleting the check.
EOF
exit 1
